Fixes getopt's reading of short options, as POSIX, glibc and musl read
them. Meant for upstream.

- The position within a cluster of options such as `-qQ` was not reset
  when a call moved on to the next argv element, so the cluster after
  it started where this one had ended: `xz -qQ -cdf` (as xzdiff runs
  it) never saw -c, and decompressed its file in place.
- An option's argument in the next element was refused when it began
  with '-', where POSIX takes it whatever it is: `grep -e -x`.
- A missing argument left optind on its element, so a program that
  went on after the error read it again, and printed no message.
- An unknown option in a cluster set optopt to the cluster's first
  character, named the whole element, and skipped the rest of it.
- ':' in argv matched the ':' of optstring, and a position left past
  the end of an element by a program that set optind itself read past
  it.

